# --- ScholarFolio environment -----------------------------------------------
# Copy to `.env` and adjust for your machine. Never commit real secrets.

# PostgreSQL 15+ connection string.
#
# Supabase direct host (recommended — the pooler host does not negotiate TLS
# from every network):
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:<password>@db.<ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres?schema=public&sslmode=require"
#
# Supabase session-mode pooler (needs the `postgres.<ref>` username form):
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?schema=public&sslmode=require"
#
# Local native install:
#
# NOTE ON `$` IN A PASSWORD, which is the single most expensive thing in this
# file to get wrong. Two different parsers read this file and they disagree:
#
#   * Next.js loads `.env` through `@next/env`, which runs `dotenv-expand`. A bare
#     `$` starts a variable reference, so `Anirban$2005` expands to `Anirban` and
#     the app authenticates with a truncated password.
#   * The Prisma CLI loads the same file with plain `dotenv`, which does no
#     expansion. So the fix of writing `Anirban\$2005` — correct for Next — is
#     passed to Postgres as a literal backslash and rejected.
#
# There is no spelling of a bare `$` that satisfies both, and the failure is
# silent in the worst way: the app and the CLI each work in isolation, so the
# credentials look correct right up until the one that is wrong is used.
#
# Percent-encode it instead. `%24` is a `$` to every URI parser and contains no
# `$` for `dotenv-expand` to expand, so both readers end up with the same
# password. Do this for any reserved character in the password: `%40` for `@`,
# `%3A` for `:`, `%2F` for `/`, `%23` for `#`.
#
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:Anirban%242005@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/...
#
# NOTE ON connection_limit, which is load-bearing on a pooler and harmless
# elsewhere. Prisma sizes its pool at (CPU cores * 2 + 1) by default, so on an
# 8-core machine it opens up to 17 connections. A Supabase *session-mode* pooler
# allows far fewer in total, and a single Next server can exhaust it on its own.
# The failure is intermittent and baffling: a page that worked a moment ago now
# fails with
#
#   FATAL: (EMAXCONNSESSION) max clients reached in session mode -
#   max clients are limited to pool_size: 15
#
# which reads as a flaky database rather than as a pool the app outgrew.
# `connection_limit` caps it, and it is a latency dial as well as a safety one:
# a page that issues several independent queries can only overlap them as far as
# the pool allows, so too low a value turns a parallel page into a serial one.
# `npm run probe:pool` measures the actual ceiling on your project — 10 was the
# right value here, and 13 was where the pooler started refusing.
#
#   DATABASE_URL="...?schema=public&sslmode=require&connection_limit=10&pool_timeout=30"
#
# Local native install (a port other than 5432 if Postgres is already taken):
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:<password>@localhost:5432/scholarfolio?schema=public"
#
# Then create the database and load it:
#   npm run db:setup        # migrate deploy + seed
#
# The default below points at 5433 so it will not collide with a system Postgres
# on 5432. Change it to match your instance, or run Postgres on another port.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:<password>@localhost:5433/scholarfolio?schema=public"

# 32+ random bytes. Rotating this invalidates every existing session.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
SESSION_SECRET="replace-me-with-32-plus-random-bytes-before-deploying"

# Institution IANA timezone. Drives term day boundaries and due-date display.
APP_TIMEZONE="Asia/Kolkata"

# Public origin, used for absolute links and origin checks on mutations.
APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# "development" | "production" | "test"
NODE_ENV="development"
